## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **Superior performance in low-data, high-compression regimes**: Figure 3a shows S_convex significantly outperforms S_non-convex on SVHN at high compression ratios (filter counts 1–16), and Figure 3b shows the gap widens in the data-scarce setting (100 samples/class on CIFAR10). This directly validates the core claim that convex distillation excels where non-convex distillation struggles.

2. **Label-free compression with no post-compression fine-tuning**: The distillation loss (Eq. 6) depends only on activation matching ‖f_T(z)−f_S(z)‖², with no label dependency. All experiments (Figures 3–7, Table 2) report test accuracy after directly swapping S into the model with zero fine-tuning on labeled data, demonstrating that the convex student is inference-ready upon distillation completion.

3. **Orders-of-magnitude faster convergence with convex solvers**: Figure 5 shows RFISTA and Approximate Cone Decomposition solvers reach target accuracy ~1–2 orders of magnitude faster than both full-batch and mini-batch Adam on TinyImageNet binary classification. The convex formulation enables drop-in use of specialized solvers, a unique advantage over non-convex alternatives.

### Supporting strengths (strengthen the paper but are not its core contribution)

4. **~10× compression with preserved accuracy**: Figure 4 (rightmost subplot) demonstrates that compressing both Blocks 3 and 4 of ResNet18 achieves approximately 10× overall model compression with no significant accuracy drop on the CIFAR10 full test set, validated end-to-end with no fine-tuning.

5. **Polishing step addresses one-vs-all limitation**: Section 4.3 and Figure 7 describe a concrete mechanism — freezing W₁ from SCNN's one-vs-all solution and recomputing W₂ via group elastic regression (using the Adelie library) to enable cross-output information sharing. Figure 7 shows this polishing step enables convex distillation to match or approach non-convex performance even with relaxed resource constraints.

6. **Convex heads generalize to non-distillation settings**: Table 2 shows convex classification heads outperform non-convex heads on Visual Wake Words when the backbone is frozen (81.36 vs 80.84) and match them when trainable (83.47 vs 83.42), suggesting applicability beyond distillation to downstream fine-tuning scenarios.

7. **Fair comparison protocol**: For every experiment, S_non-convex and S_prune are controlled to have the same parameter count as S_convex (Section 5.1). The paper explicitly notes that for the same filter count, #θ_non-convex ≈ 2× #θ_convex, and adjusts non-convex hidden dimensions accordingly, ensuring apples-to-apples comparisons.

### Presentation / practical strengths

8. **Progressive experimental design**: The paper systematically escalates experimental complexity — from standard optimizer comparisons (Section 5.1), to convex solvers (Section 5.2), to polishing improvements (Section 5.3), to end-to-end fine-tuning ablation (Section 5.4) — building a coherent case layer by layer.

## Summary of what this paper does well

The paper introduces Convex Distillation, a method that compresses non-convex DNN blocks into convex (gated ReLU) blocks via activation matching, requiring no labels and no post-compression fine-tuning. The single most important piece of evidence is that convex students consistently outperform parameter-matched non-convex students in high-compression and low-data regimes (Figures 3a, 3b, 4, 6), with convex solvers reaching target accuracy 1–2 orders of magnitude faster than Adam (Figure 5). The method achieves ~10× model compression on ResNet18 without significant accuracy loss (Figure 4) while being entirely label-free, addressing a practical gap left by distillation methods that require labeled fine-tuning.

